#Problem APEX 4.1.14

## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Applications of differentiation)
## DBsection(Newton's method)
## KEYWORDS("Newton's method", 'rootfinding')
## Institution(Valdosta State University)
## Author(S. V. Ault)
## Level(3)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('APEX Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Hartman')
## EditionText1('3.0')
## Section1('4.1')
## Problem1('14')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'plots.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');
Context('Numeric')->flags->set(    tolerance => 0.0001,    tolType   => "absolute");

$a     = random(2, 5);
$f1_gr = "exp(x^2)";
$f2_gr = "$a cos(x)";

$f  = Formula("exp(x^2) - $a cos(x)");
$df = $f->D('x');

@xa = (-1);
for my $k (0..4) { push(@xa, $xa[$k] - $f->eval(x => $xa[$k])/$df->eval(x=>$xa[$k]));}

@xb = (1);
for my $k (0..4) { push(@xb, $xb[$k] - $f->eval(x => $xb[$k])/$df->eval(x=>$xb[$k]));}

$ans = List($xa[-1], $xb[-1]);

$plot = Plot(xmin => -2, xmax => 2, xtick_delta => 1, xminor => 0, rounded_corners => 1);
$plot->add_function(["e^(x^2)",'x',-2,2, color => 'blue'],["$a*cos(x)",'x',-2,2,color => 'green']);

$altText =
    "A graph with two curves. One of the curves looks like a parabola with "
    . "a vertex at (0,1) and opening up.  The other looks like a wider parabola "
    . "opening down, with vertext at (0, $a).";

BEGIN_PGML
Use Newton's Method to approximate all 
when the given functions are equal.  In other
words, solve [`f(x) = g(x)`].

Provide accuracy to 4 places after the decimal.  Use
the graph below to obtain good initial approximations.

[```  
   f(x) = e^{x^2}, \quad g(x) = [$a] \cos x
```]

>> [![$altText]!]{$plot}{300} <<

[`f(x) = g(x)`] when [`x = `] [_]{$ans}{30}

(Separate multiple answers by commas.)
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Solving [`f(x) = g(x)`] is equivalent to finding the roots
of [`h(x) = f(x) - g(x)`].

Set up the iterative formula for Newton's Method:

[```
  x_{n+1} = x_n - \dfrac{h(x_n)}{h'(x_n)} 
  = x_n - \dfrac{e^{x_n^2} - [$a] \cos x_n}{2x_n e^{x_n^2} + [$a] \sin x_n}
```]

There are two roots.  The smaller one is near [`x = [$xa[0]]`].
Using Newton's Method,
[```
  \begin{aligned}
    x_0 &= [$xa[0]] \\
    x_1 &\approx [$xa[1]] \\
    x_2 &\approx [$xa[2]] \\
    x_3 &\approx [$xa[3]] \\
    x_4 &\approx [$xa[4]]
  \end{aligned}
```]

The larger root is near [`x = [$xb[0]]`].
Using Newton's Method,
[```
  \begin{aligned}
    x_0 &= [$xb[0]] \\
    x_1 &\approx [$xb[1]] \\
    x_2 &\approx [$xb[2]] \\
    x_3 &\approx [$xb[3]] \\
    x_4 &\approx [$xb[4]]
  \end{aligned}
```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION
ENDDOCUMENT();

